lesson chemistry-rust-on-the-lock-gates v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=3 subject=chemistry levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Rust on the lock gates"
  goal "Say what rust needs, choose the way to stop it for each part of the lock, and work out what the rust weighs."

  look {
    scene 32x9 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 0) space=2 align=end {
        canallock lock level=0.5 boat=1
        nails jars jars=["water", "salt", "painted"] days=10 letters=1 names=1
      }
    }
    say """
      The lock gates are held together with iron. Iron rusts only when water and air reach it
      together, and faster when the water is salty. Rust is a new substance: oxygen from the air has
      joined the iron, so the rust weighs more than the iron did. The canal stops it in three ways.
      Paint keeps water and air off the straps, grease keeps them off the cogs that rub, and a coat
      of zinc wears away first, even where it is scratched.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice chem.rust-pair count=2 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=3
    level easy {
      worked chem.rust-weighs d=0 i=140 level=medium
    }
    show chem.rust-weighs d=0 i=350 easy-i=70 hard-d=1 hard-i=280
    practice chem.rust-rate count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice chem.stop-rust-which count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=2
    level hard {
      show chem.rust-pair o=2 t=1
    }
  }
  story {
    show chem.rust-weighs d=1 i=140 easy-d=0 easy-i=280 hard-d=2 hard-i=280
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show chem.scratched-coat k=1 r=0 easy-k=0 easy-r=2 hard-r=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show chem.paint-or-zinc p=20 y=6 z=150 easy-y=10 easy-z=90 hard-y=5 hard-p=30 hard-z=120
  }
  remember {
    say "Iron rusts when water and air reach it together. Paint, grease and zinc keep it from rusting, and zinc works even when scratched."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that rust needs three things at once (iron, water and oxygen from the air), so every
    way of stopping it takes one away: paint and grease cover the iron, and zinc, the coat on
    galvanised gates and railings, corrodes before the iron does, which is why a scratched zinc coat
    still protects. The jars use the grade four test (dry air, water under oil, salty water); what is
    new here is choosing the one pair that tests a given claim, and measuring how much faster salt
    makes it: the table of steel lost a year uses figures inside the international bands for
    carbon steel (ISO 9223), about 20 thousandths of a millimetre inland, 40 in a town and 160 in
    salt spray. The weights use the real ratio for
    dry iron oxide, 7 g of iron to 10 g of oxide; real rust also holds water, so it weighs a little
    more again. The wrong ideas to watch for are that rust was under the paint all along, and that
    rusting makes iron lighter. The three-star question compares two plans over 30 years with prices
    from the lock keeper's notes. If the child picks any pair with plain water in it, stay here
    another day and ask what is different between the two jars before asking what it shows. Off the
    page, look for rust on a gate or a bike and for a galvanised bin or railing, which is dull grey
    with a pattern like frost.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the rust's weight is worked once in the teacher's pen, the pair to find tests water or salt, the pin weighs 70 g and the rust is asked, the salt table asks how many times as fast, the ways to stop rust are the cogs and the paint, the two-star asks why the nail under oil stays bright, and paint lasts 10 years."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the pair to find tests air or salt, the oxygen that joined and the iron before rusting are worked back, the salt table gives the years a thin sign lasts, the ways to stop rust are the two about zinc, the two-star asks why only the painted strap rusted in its scratch, where zinc corroding first is the answer, and paint lasts only 5 years."
  }
}
